      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The anti-gravity treadmill is one of the few pieces of rehabilitation equipment most
          people have heard of without ever seeing one. It appears in stories about professional
          athletes coming back from injury and in physical therapy clinic windows, usually under
          the AlterG name. It is not science fiction, and it is not zero gravity: it is a
          treadmill inside a sealed, inflatable chamber that lifts you by part of your body weight.
          This page explains how it works, who uses one, how to get access, what sessions and
          machines typically cost, and the home options for people who cannot get to a clinic.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What Is an Anti-Gravity Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>An anti-gravity treadmill is a treadmill enclosed in a sealed air chamber from
            the waist down; raising the air pressure in the chamber lifts the user, so they walk or
            run carrying only a set percentage of their body weight.</strong> The best-known make
            is the AlterG Anti-Gravity Treadmill, whose current models can unweight a user by up to
            80 percent of body weight in one-percent steps, according to the manufacturer.
          </p>
          <p>
            The technique is called differential air pressure. You wear a pair of special shorts
            with a skirt that zips into an opening in the chamber, the chamber inflates, and the
            pressure difference pushes upward on your body. Because the lift is spread evenly,
            your gait stays close to normal, which is the point: you can practise walking or
            running with near-normal mechanics while your joints, a healing bone or a repaired
            ligament carry less load.
          </p>
          <p>
            These machines are mainly found in rehabilitation hospitals, outpatient physical
            therapy clinics, university sports programmes and professional teams. Clinics that
            sell sessions directly to the public commonly charge somewhere in the region of $15 to
            $75 per session on the US price lists we checked, depending on the package and the
            location. Buying a clinical machine costs tens of thousands of dollars. A small number
            of home-focused air-pressure treadmills now exist, and for most people the practical
            home alternatives are low-impact walking set-ups rather than unweighting machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How Does Differential Air Pressure Work?</h2>
<p>
            Differential air pressure works by raising the air pressure around your lower body
            slightly above the pressure around your upper body, so that the difference produces an
            upward force that offsets part of your weight.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, the treadmill sits inside a flexible chamber, a little like a large
            inflatable bag sealed around the frame. The user steps into an opening in the top,
            wearing neoprene shorts with a fabric skirt around the waist. The skirt zips onto the
            opening and forms an airtight seal at the waist. A blower then raises the pressure in
            the chamber. Because the user's lower body is inside the higher-pressure zone and the
            upper body is outside it, the net effect is an upward push at the waist, and the
            treadmill's scale reads a lower effective body weight.
          </p>
          <p>
            The machine first calibrates to your weight, then the operator sets a percentage, for
            example 60 percent of body weight. The manufacturer states that the current AlterG
            systems adjust the pressure continuously and in one-percent increments, and allow up to
            80 percent unweighting, which leaves the user carrying 20 percent of body weight at the
            lowest setting. From there the session is ordinary treadmill walking or running at a
            set speed and incline, with the unweighting changed as the session or the rehabilitation
            programme progresses.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why air rather than a harness?</strong> Harness-based body-weight-support
            systems also exist and are widely used in rehabilitation, particularly for people who
            need help with balance. A harness lifts from the trunk and groin, which can be
            uncomfortable at higher unweighting and can alter the way the hips move. Air pressure
            lifts more evenly around the pelvis and leaves the arms and legs free, which is why
            runners and athletes tend to use it. The two do different jobs, and a clinic will choose
            between them for the patient.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The NASA connection.</strong> The idea came from research at NASA's Ames
            Research Center, where Robert Whalen worked on using air pressure to load astronauts'
            bodies during exercise. NASA's own account describes how the concept was reversed to
            reduce load instead, and later developed into the AlterG. It is a genuine spin-off,
            though in day-to-day use it is a rehabilitation and training tool rather than space
            technology.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alterg-today" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Is the AlterG, and Who Makes It Now?</h2>
<p>
            AlterG is the brand name most people mean when they search for an alter g treadmill,
            and since August 2023 it has been owned by Lifeward, the company formerly called ReWalk
            Robotics.
          </p>
          <p>
            ReWalk completed its purchase of AlterG on 11 August 2023, for a cash price of
            approximately $19 million according to its annual report, and later renamed the
            combined company Lifeward. AlterG's own site, checked on 14 October 2026, lists three
            current models, the NEO, the NEO+ and the PRO, all built around the same
            differential-air-pressure technology. Lifeward's most recent annual report describes
            the NEO as the entry-level model introduced in 2024, the NEO+ as adding higher speeds
            and an integrated camera for gait analysis, and the PRO as the top model aimed at sports
            medicine and elite sport. Older models such as the M320, F320, Via 400 and Pro 200
            remain in many clinics, and you may be booked on any of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Lifeward's annual report states that AlterG systems are used in more than 6,000
            facilities in more than 40 countries. It describes its markets as inpatient
            rehabilitation facilities, outpatient clinics and sports programmes, and it does not
            describe a consumer home model. Users wear proprietary shorts, which some clinics sell
            to patients and others lend.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Is it the same as a zero gravity treadmill?</strong> In searches, "zero gravity
            treadmill" usually means the same thing. No treadmill removes gravity, and the clinical
            machines stop well short of zero body weight; the term is marketing shorthand for
            reduced-body-weight running.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-uses-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Who Uses an Anti-Gravity Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Anti-gravity treadmills are used mainly by people in physical rehabilitation after
            lower-limb injury or surgery, by people with neurological conditions that affect
            walking, by athletes returning from injury or managing training load, and by people
            for whom full-weight walking is painful.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>After lower-limb injury or surgery.</strong> Clinics use reduced body
            weight to let patients walk or run earlier and with better mechanics than they could at
            full weight, following the surgeon's or therapist's loading plan. Typical examples are
            knee and hip surgery, ankle injuries and stress fractures.</li>
            <li><strong>Neurological conditions.</strong> The Shirley Ryan AbilityLab in Chicago,
            for example, lists stroke, multiple sclerosis, Guillain-Barre syndrome and spinal cord
            injury among the conditions its AlterG training serves, alongside general strength,
            mobility and balance goals.</li>
            <li><strong>Runners and athletes.</strong> Professional and college teams use
            anti-gravity treadmills to keep injured athletes running while a bone or tendon heals,
            and some runners use them to add training volume with less impact.</li>
            <li><strong>Joint pain and higher body weight.</strong> Some clinics market sessions to
            people who want to walk for longer with less joint stress, including older adults and
            people whose weight makes walking uncomfortable.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Who it is not for, or not yet.</strong> Clinics screen users before the first
            session, and some conditions, recent wounds, or surgical restrictions rule it out or
            delay it. The Shirley Ryan AbilityLab, for example, states that clients must be
            prescreened. If you are under the care of a surgeon or therapist, the decision about
            whether and when to use one, and at what body weight, is theirs to make with you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-access" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Do You Get a Session on One?</h2>
<p>
            Most people use an anti-gravity treadmill either as part of a physical therapy plan,
            where the therapist includes it in treatment, or by booking cash-pay sessions at a
            clinic or sports facility that offers them to the public.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Through physical therapy</h3>
          <p>
            If you are already in a course of physical therapy, ask whether the clinic has one and
            whether it suits your plan. When it is used as part of treatment, it is part of the
            therapy session rather than a separate purchase, and whether insurance covers that
            session depends on your plan and the clinic's billing, so ask both.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cash-pay sessions</h3>
          <p>
            Many clinics and hospital wellness centres rent time on the machine directly, often
            in 30-minute blocks, with an initial screening or orientation. The AlterG site has a
            location finder listing facilities with its machines, which is the simplest place to
            start; then call the facility and ask whether it offers self-pay sessions, whether a
            referral is needed, and whether a staff member supervises.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What a session is like</h3>
          <p>
            Expect to put on the shorts, step into the chamber and zip in, then stand while the
            machine calibrates to your weight. The operator sets a body-weight percentage and you
            walk or run as you would on any treadmill, with the speed, incline and unweighting
            adjusted during the session. People commonly describe the first minutes as odd, a
            feeling of being lifted at the hips, before settling into it. Sessions are usually
            30 minutes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="session-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. How Much Does an Anti-Gravity Treadmill Session Cost?</h2>
<p>
            On the US clinic price lists we checked on 14 October 2026, cash-pay anti-gravity
            treadmill sessions ranged from about $12.50 to $75 per session, depending on the
            facility and whether you buy a single session, a package or a membership.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
            <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 text-left">
                  <th class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200">Facility (as published)</th>
                  <th class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200">Single session</th>
                  <th class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200">Package or membership</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Ogden Clinic, Utah</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$25 for 30 minutes</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$16 per session on a 10-session pass; $150 a month for three 30-minute sessions a week</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Shirley Ryan AbilityLab, Chicago</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$75</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$700 for 10 sessions ($70 each)</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            These are two published examples at opposite ends of the range, not a national
            average, and they cover different settings: a clinic renting time on the machine, and
            a specialist rehabilitation centre programme. Prices change and vary widely by city,
            so treat the band as a guide and call your local provider. In the UK, private
            providers commonly publish prices in the region of £20 to £45 per session, again
            varying with packages.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Extra costs to ask about:</strong> an initial assessment or orientation, which
            some providers require and charge for separately; the special shorts, which some
            clinics sell and others lend; and whether a therapist supervises, which may cost more
            than unsupervised time on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Insurance.</strong> When an anti-gravity treadmill is used within a course of
            physical therapy, the session is generally billed as therapy, and whether it is covered
            depends on your plan. Cash-pay wellness sessions are usually not covered. Ask the clinic
            how it bills before the first session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How Much Does It Cost to Buy an Anti-Gravity Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Clinical anti-gravity treadmills cost tens of thousands of dollars, and the
            manufacturer does not publish list prices; facilities buy them through sales
            representatives and distributors, often with financing and service contracts.
          </p>
          <p>
            The figures that do exist come from press coverage over the years, so treat them as
            historical rather than current. Engadget reported an earlier, smaller AlterG model at
            $24,500 and up, around 2010, with a monthly financing option. Local news coverage of
            clinics buying one has quoted figures in the region of $35,000 to $75,000 depending on
            model. Lifeward's annual report, checked on 14 October 2026, does not disclose prices,
            and notes that it also earns revenue from the shorts users wear and from extended
            service contracts after the standard warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            Beyond the purchase, a clinical machine needs floor space, a suitable electrical
            supply, installation, staff training and ongoing service. That is why most private
            individuals use one at a clinic rather than buying.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Home-focused air-pressure treadmills</h3>
          <p>
            A small number of companies have tried to bring the technique home. Boost Treadmills,
            a California company founded in 2017, was set up specifically to make an affordable
            air-pressure treadmill, and NASA's spin-off coverage describes it as cutting the price
            of an antigravity treadmill by almost two-thirds. Boost's own site, checked on
            14 October 2026, describes its Boost 3 as designed for home use and available to rent
            or lease as well as buy, but does not publish a price. One press report put the Boost 3
            at $24,999 to buy, with home rental from $499 a month; we could not confirm those figures
            on Boost's own site, so ask the company for a current quote. Even at that level, it is
            a specialist purchase for people with a long-term need, usually made with a clinician's
            input.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="evidence-and-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Can an Anti-Gravity Treadmill Do, and What Can It Not?</h2>
<p>
            An anti-gravity treadmill lets someone walk or run with less load on the legs while
            keeping a near-normal gait, which is genuinely useful in rehabilitation; it does not
            replace the rest of a rehabilitation programme or make full-weight training
            unnecessary.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it does well.</strong> It allows graded loading: a therapist can start a
            patient at a low percentage of body weight and raise it session by session. It lets
            athletes maintain running fitness and the habit of running while an injury heals. It
            allows people who find walking painful to walk for longer, which can build confidence
            and conditioning. Because the speed and unweighting are precise, progress is easy to
            measure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it does not do.</strong> It does not strengthen muscles for full body
            weight on its own; strength work and eventually full-weight walking and running are
            still part of a plan. It does not train balance in the same way as walking unsupported,
            because the chamber gives some stability at the waist. And the reduction in load is not
            the only thing that matters in healing: timing, the type of injury and the surgeon's
            restrictions all count.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are not in a position to summarise the clinical research on outcomes, and we do not
            quote it. If you want to know whether it would help your particular recovery, ask your
            surgeon or physical therapist, who can weigh it against the alternatives for your
            condition.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Are the Home Alternatives?</h2>
<p>
            For most people the realistic home alternatives are low-impact rather than reduced
            body weight: walking at a gentle pace on a well-cushioned treadmill, moderate incline
            walking instead of running, pool walking, and non-impact machines such as bikes and
            ellipticals. None of them unweights you, but each reduces impact in its own way.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A cushioned treadmill at walking pace.</strong> A deck with effective
            cushioning softens footstrike compared with pavement. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cushioning</a>
            explains what it does and does not change, and
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our picks for bad knees</a>
            covers machines suited to joint-conscious walkers.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline walking instead of running.</strong> Walking uphill raises the
            effort without the impact of running. Our guide to
            <a href="/what-incline-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what incline to walk on</a>
            explains how to start. Incline does increase load on some structures, such as the
            Achilles and calves, so check with your therapist if those are the injured part.</li>
            <li><strong>Pool walking and deep-water running.</strong> Water is the original
            unweighting: buoyancy reduces load, and deep-water running uses a flotation belt to
            remove impact altogether. Many community pools allow it, and some physical therapy
            clinics have pools with built-in underwater treadmills.</li>
            <li><strong>Bikes and ellipticals.</strong> Both remove impact. Our comparison of a
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs elliptical</a>
            sets out the trade-offs.</li>
            <li><strong>Handrail support, sparingly.</strong> <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Holding the rails</a> takes some weight
            off the legs, and in early rehabilitation a therapist may suggest it. It also changes
            posture and gait, so it is a stopgap rather than a substitute for unweighting.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you need true reduced-body-weight training at home for a long-term condition, talk
            to your therapist about whether a home air-pressure machine, a rental, or regular clinic
            sessions make most sense. For most short recoveries, clinic sessions during the
            critical weeks and a low-impact set-up at home are the usual combination.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="questions-to-ask" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What Should You Ask Before Booking?</h2>
<p>
            Before booking anti-gravity treadmill sessions, confirm that they fit your treatment
            plan, what the full cost will be, and who will supervise.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Has my surgeon or therapist approved it, and at what body weight should I
            start?</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Is a screening or assessment required, and is it charged
            separately?</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Will a therapist supervise, or is it self-guided after the
            orientation?</strong></li>
            <li><strong>What does a single session cost, and what do packages or memberships
            cost?</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Do I need to buy the shorts, or does the clinic provide them?</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Is it billed as therapy, and will my insurance cover any of it?</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Which model does the clinic have, and does it record gait data I can take
            to my therapist?</strong></li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            A provider that will not screen you, or that encourages you to go beyond your
            surgeon's restrictions, is not the right one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is an anti-gravity treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a treadmill enclosed in a sealed air chamber from the waist down. You wear special shorts that zip into the chamber, the air pressure inside is raised, and the pressure difference lifts you so that you walk or run carrying only a set percentage of your body weight. The AlterG is the best-known make.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an AlterG treadmill the same as a zero gravity treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In practice, yes: zero gravity treadmill is a common search term for the same kind of machine. It is a misnomer, because no treadmill removes gravity. AlterG states that its current models can unweight a user by up to 80 percent of body weight, so the lowest setting still leaves you carrying about a fifth of your weight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does an AlterG session cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On the US clinic price lists we checked in October 2026, cash-pay sessions ranged from about $12.50 to $75 each depending on the facility and whether you buy one session, a package or a membership. An initial assessment and the special shorts may cost extra. Within physical therapy, it is usually billed as part of the therapy session.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you buy an anti-gravity treadmill for home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clinical machines such as the AlterG are sold to facilities and cost tens of thousands of dollars. A few home-focused air-pressure treadmills exist; Boost Treadmills offers its Boost 3 to buy, rent or lease for home use but does not publish a price on its site. For most people, clinic sessions plus a low-impact home set-up are more practical.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does insurance cover anti-gravity treadmill therapy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on your plan and how the clinic bills. When the machine is used as part of a prescribed course of physical therapy, the session is generally billed as therapy and may be covered on the same terms as other therapy visits. Cash-pay wellness sessions are usually not covered. Ask both the clinic and your insurer before you start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who should not use an anti-gravity treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That is a decision for your surgeon or physical therapist. Clinics screen users before the first session, and some injuries, wounds, surgical restrictions or medical conditions rule it out or delay it. If you are recovering from surgery or injury, do not book sessions without your treating clinician's agreement on whether and at what body weight to start.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Anti-Gravity Treadmills</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The machine is simple to use. The mistakes are about when, how much, and how it fits into a recovery.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Booking sessions without your clinician</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Reduced body weight makes it easy to do more than a healing injury can take. If you are recovering from surgery or injury, agree the starting body weight, speed and progression with your surgeon or physical therapist before booking, and tell the clinic what your restrictions are.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating it as a substitute for rehabilitation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Walking or running at reduced body weight is one tool. Strength work, balance training and a gradual return to full-weight walking or running are still needed, and the machine does not do those for you.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing session prices without the extras</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A cheap session can come with a separate assessment fee, a requirement to buy the shorts, or no supervision. Compare the total cost of the number of sessions you expect to need, including what is and is not supervised.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a home treadmill to do the same job</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A cushioned home treadmill reduces impact compared with pavement, but it does not reduce body weight. If your plan calls for unweighting, you need a clinic session, a harness system or a specialist air-pressure machine, not just a softer deck.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

